## Market read: prices held steady

[Matched-coffee prices were flat this week](https://api.purveyors.io/v1/price-index/stats).

## Traceability: ask for the evidence behind the label

A [Mongabay report republished by Daily Coffee News](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/03/ecuadors-deforestation-free-coffee-model-moves-beyond-the-pilot-stage/) describes Ecuador’s Deforestation-Free Coffee Initiative: 373 participating producers, farm-boundary mapping, forest-cover checks, and independent verification. The initiative exported 172.5 metric tons between 2022 and 2025.

For buyers evaluating a deforestation-free claim, the useful questions are specific: **Can the supplier connect this lot to mapped farms? What verification is available, and who maintains it?** Scaling still depends on technical assistance. Ask for the lot-level documentation before paying a premium for the label.

## Cafe operators: watch the leases, not an opening count

[7 Brew won an approximately $143.2 million bankruptcy auction](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/02/7-brew-wins-143-million-auction-for-former-salad-and-go-locations/) for assets including 73 former Salad and Go leases: 41 in Arizona, 20 in Texas, and six each in Nevada and Oklahoma. **The transaction awaits bankruptcy court approval.**

For operators in those markets, track which nearby leases clear court approval and move into conversion: those sites—not the headline total—are the competitive watchlist.

## Off the cupping table

[Coffee People Zine is returning with Issue 26](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/04/coffee-people-zine-back-in-print-plans-minneapolis-party/): more than 150 pages from more than 80 contributors around the theme “enough.” DCN reports a public launch party on **September 20, 6:30–9:30 p.m., at FRGMNT Coffee inside Open Book in Minneapolis**.
